using System.Collections.Frozen;

namespace Surl.Cli;

/// <summary>
/// The hand-written paragraphs of <c>surl --aihelp</c> (ADR-0046 decision 6): the overview's
/// <c>About</c>, <c>Command line</c> and <c>Conventions</c> and each topic's <c>About</c>, one
/// line per paragraph or list item. Each statement is true of the code it describes, and says it
/// as <see cref="ManualText"/> does where both say the same thing; work that changes that code
/// updates the paragraph in the same change.
/// </summary>
internal static class AiHelpProse
{
    /// <summary>The overview's <c>About</c> paragraphs.</summary>
    public static IReadOnlyList<string> OverviewAbout { get; } =
    [
        "surl is the server-side mate of curl: for each request upstream curl makes, surl is the server that answers it. Where `curl [options] <url>` names what to fetch, `surl [options] <url>` names what to listen on.",
        "`surl --version` names the schemes this build serves. Each protocol has a topic below with its schemes, their default ports, its options and an upstream curl command line that reaches it.",
        "surl serves until it is stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and then exits 0.",
        OverviewHosts,
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// The overview's paragraph pointing at the two other hosts of the same servers, <c>Surl.Api</c> and
    /// <c>Surl.Mcp</c>, and where their own help lives (ADR-0099 decision 9, ADR-0103 decision 6).
    /// </summary>
    public const string OverviewHosts =
        "The same servers start in-process from .NET through the `Surl.Api` package: `SurlServer.StartAsync` takes a "
        + "`SurlServerOptions`, whose properties are these options, and its help is the package README and XML documentation. "
        + "An MCP client starts them through `surl-mcp`, the `Surl.Mcp` executable, on standard input and output: its tools "
        + "`start_server`, `list_servers`, `stop_server` and `get_exchanges` describe themselves, and it serves these pages as "
        + "the `surl://aihelp` resources.";

    /// <summary>The overview's <c>Command line</c> paragraphs.</summary>
    public static IReadOnlyList<string> OverviewCommandLine { get; } =
    [
        "A command line is `surl [options] <url>...`, with at least one listen URL (see the listen-urls topic).",
        "Options and listen URLs may come in any order and are read left to right; the first error ends the reading, and nothing is served. After `--` every argument is a listen URL.",
        "A long option's argument is the next argument (`--max-time 30`) or follows an equals sign (`--max-time=30`).",
        "Short options may be bundled: `-vs` is `-v -s`. An option that takes an argument ends the bundle and takes the rest of it as its argument, so `-vm30` is `-v -m 30`; with nothing left, it takes the next argument.",
        "A flag whose Allowed values say `--no-<name> turns it off` is turned off with `--no-<name>`, such as `--no-verbose`.",
        "An option or argument the command line refuses writes `surl: option --<name>: <reason>` to stderr, then `surl: try 'surl --help' or 'surl --manual' for more information`, and exits 2.",
    ];

    /// <summary>The overview's <c>Conventions</c> paragraphs.</summary>
    public static IReadOnlyList<string> OverviewConventions { get; } =
    [
        "Every topic page has the same five sections, in this order: About, Schemes, Options, Exit codes and Examples. A section with nothing in it holds the one line `Nothing for this topic.`.",
        "In the option table, Option is how the option is written; Argument type and Allowed values say what its argument may be; Default is its value when it is not given (`not applicable` when it has none); Loosens security says `yes, for tests only` for an option that loosens a secure default and writes a warning, `yes, widens what a peer may do` for one that exposes more, and `no` otherwise; Categories names every topic that lists it; Description is its `surl --help` line.",
        "In the exit-code table, Code is the process exit code, Name its name in surl's source, Meaning when surl returns it, and What to do next the step that fixes it.",
        "In an example, `<port>` stands for the port surl bound and `<path>` for a directory; substitute them. An example that serves keeps serving until it is stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and then exits 0.",
        "`surl --help`, `surl --manual`, `surl --version` and these pages are written to stdout at every log level, `-s` included.",
    ];

    /// <summary>Each topic's <c>About</c> paragraphs, by topic name.</summary>
    public static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<string>> TopicAbout { get; } = new Dictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<string>>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        ["auth"] =
        [
            "`-u, --user <user:password>` adds one account, and may be given again to add more. The value is split at its first colon, so a user name never holds one. An empty user name makes the password a Bearer token. A user name given twice is refused.",
            "`--user-file <file>` reads accounts from a file, after every `--user` account. The file is UTF-8 (a byte-order mark is skipped), with LF or CR LF line ends; each line is one `user:password`, read as `--user` reads its value, and blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped. On Linux and macOS, a file whose mode lets the group or other users read it is warned at every start with `surl: warning: <file> is readable by other users and may hold passwords` (`chmod 600` the file to silence it); Windows has no such warning. A file that cannot be read exits 37, and a malformed line exits 2, naming the line.",
            "`--group <name>=<member>,...` names a group of accounts, and may be given again for more groups. Each member is an account name from `--user` or `--user-file`, or `@` and another group's name, so groups nest; an account reached twice counts once. A field percent-encodes `%`, `;`, `=` in the name and `,` in a member, as URLs do. No option takes a member list yet, so today a group is only checked at start and nothing uses it: `surl --group admins=alice,bob -u alice:secret -u bob:secret http://127.0.0.1:8080/` starts as it would without `--group`. A group name given twice or a member named twice in one group exits 2 as the command line is read. At start, before any listener binds, surl exits 2 with `surl: (2) option --group: member <member> names no account or group` for a member naming neither, `surl: (2) option --group: group <name> includes itself` for a group that includes itself directly or through other groups, `surl: (2) option --group: group everyone is reserved` for a group named everyone and `surl: (2) option --group: member @everyone is not allowed here` for that member. With `-v`, surl notes `* Group <name>: <n> accounts` for each group.",
            "`--keytab <file>` reads Kerberos service keys from an MIT keytab file (version 0x0502, as `ktutil`, `kadmin ktadd` and Windows' `ktpass /out` write it) when surl starts, before any listener binds. A key serves the principal the keytab names it for, `<service>/<host>@<REALM>`, where the service is `HTTP` for http and https, `smtp`, `imap`, `pop` or `ldap`. Only the AES keys are kept (aes128-cts-hmac-sha1-96, aes256-cts-hmac-sha1-96, aes128-cts-hmac-sha256-128, aes256-cts-hmac-sha384-192); each key of another enctype, such as rc4-hmac, is skipped with `surl: warning: --keytab: skipped the <enctype> key of <principal>`. A file that cannot be read writes `surl: (37) Could not read keytab <file>` and exits 37; one that is not a version 0x0502 keytab or is cut short writes `surl: (2) Keytab <file> is malformed at byte <offset>`, and one with no AES key `surl: (2) Keytab <file> holds no key surl can use`, each exiting 2. `--auth gssapi` needs `--keytab`: without it a start writes `surl: (2) --auth gssapi needs --keytab` and exits 2. When `--auth` names neither negotiate nor gssapi (its default names neither), surl writes `surl: warning: --keytab is unused: --auth accepts neither negotiate nor gssapi`. No key byte is ever logged. HTTP `negotiate` accepts a Kerberos ticket under the keys, bare or inside SPNEGO, answering with the AP-REP when the client asks for mutual authentication, the SMTP, IMAP and POP3 servers' SASL `GSSAPI` logins use them too, and so does the LDAP server's `GSS-SPNEGO` bind (see the ldap topic). A Kerberos login is the ticket's client principal, written `user@EXAMPLE.COM`, and is accepted only when an account of exactly that name exists; its password is not used.",
            "Other local users can read `--user` in the process list, and the `--user-file` passwords are in clear: outside a test, use `--user-file` and let only the user surl runs as read it.",
            "Unless `--allow-anonymous` is given: with no account configured, an HTTP GET or HEAD needs no login, and every login is refused; once any account is configured, every HTTP request needs a login; any other HTTP method, every MQTT CONNECT, every SMTP MAIL, every IMAP command that reads or changes a mailbox, every POP3 maildrop command, every FTP login and every SMB session setup always needs one.",
            "A password or token sent in clear over an unencrypted connection (HTTP Basic or Bearer over `http://`, an MQTT password over `mqtt://`, SMTP PLAIN or LOGIN over `smtp://` and IMAP LOGIN, PLAIN or LOGIN over `imap://` without STARTTLS, POP3 USER and PASS, PLAIN or LOGIN over `pop3://` without STLS, an FTP password over `ftp://` without AUTH TLS, an LDAP simple bind over `ldap://` without StartTLS) is refused unchecked unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given, which is for tests only. Digest sends no password in clear, so `curl --digest -u user:password` logs in over `http://`; over `https://` and `mqtts://` every method is accepted.",
            "`--auth <methods>` sets the methods accepted and offered: negotiate, ntlm, digest, basic, bearer and aws-sigv4 for HTTP, and for SMTP, IMAP and POP3 logins each SASL mechanism by its lower-case name, as curl's login option `AUTH=<mech>` names it (gssapi, scram-sha-256, scram-sha-512, scram-sha-1, ntlm, digest-md5, cram-md5, plain, login, oauthbearer, xoauth2, external), plus apop for POP3's APOP, and ntlmv1 for SMB's session setup (see the smb topic), which accepting ntlm never accepts. Without it they are digest, cram-md5, basic, plain, login, bearer, oauthbearer, xoauth2, external and aws-sigv4. `external` logs in as the TLS client certificate `--cacert` verifies, so it is offered only on a connection that sent one. `gssapi` logs in with a Kerberos ticket checked against the `--keytab` keys (see `--keytab`), and is offered first, before every other mechanism. It is a loosening option for tests (see the testing topic).",
            "The SSH server (see the ssh topic) checks a password or keyboard-interactive login against the accounts, never refused as plain text since SSH encrypts first, and a public-key login against `--authorized-keys <user:file>`: the OpenSSH `authorized_keys` file whose public keys `<user>` may log in with, repeatable for other users. The value is split at its first colon, and a user given twice is refused. A file that cannot be read writes `surl: (37) Could not read authorized keys <file>` and exits 37, and a line that is not a key exits 2, naming the line.",
            "`--hostkey <file>` names one SSH host private key and `--hostcert <file>` one OpenSSH host certificate for a `--hostkey` key (see the ssh topic), each repeatable; `--pass <phrase>` decrypts a `--hostkey` key as it does a `--key`.",
        ],
        ["content"] =
        [
            "Without `--directory`, surl serves an in-memory file system. It starts empty, holds at most 256 MiB, touches no disk, and is lost at exit. Uploads into it still need `--allow-uploads`.",
            "`--directory <path>` serves the files under `<path>` and keeps service state under `<path>/.surl/`. The `.surl` folder is never served, even with `--serve-dot-files`: a read of it is answered as a missing entry, a listing leaves it out, and an upload into it is refused.",
            "While it runs, surl holds a lock on `<path>/.surl/lock`, so one surl at a time serves a data directory; a second one started on it writes `surl: (124) Directory <path> is in use by another surl process` and exits 124. The lock file stays at exit, and the operating system releases the lock when surl ends, even when it is killed.",
            "A path that is missing, not a directory or unreadable writes `surl: (37) Could not open directory <path>` and exits 37; surl does not create it. A `.surl` folder or lock file that cannot be created exits 23, so a read-only directory cannot be served.",
            "Every protocol reads the one served root: HTTP, FTP, Gopher, RTSP, SMB, TFTP, SCP, SFTP, WebSocket and S3 serve its files, and DICT reads the files directly in it as definitions. With `--directory`, the MQTT server keeps its retained messages on the message bus in `<path>/.surl/bus/retained` and the SMTP, IMAP and POP3 servers share the mail store in `<path>/.surl/mail`, and the LDAP server reads its directory from `<path>/.surl/ldap/directory.ldif`; without it, the retained messages and the mail live in memory only and the directory is empty.",
            "Uploads, directory listings, symbolic links and names that start with a dot are off by default; `--allow-uploads` (FTP, HTTP PUT, RTSP, SMB, TFTP, SCP, SFTP and S3 writes), `--list-directories` (FTP listings, Gopher menus, SFTP directory reads, WebSocket listings, S3 ListObjectsV2 and ListBuckets), `--follow-symlinks` and `--serve-dot-files` turn each on. The HTTP server answers a directory with 404 Not Found, listings on or off.",
            "`--share <name>=<path>[;readonly][;protocols=<scheme>,...]` serves the folder `<path>` on disk as the top-level folder `<name>` of the served files, and may be given again for more shares. `<path>` is read as `--directory`'s is and must be an existing directory, with or without `--directory`; surl creates none, and writes `surl: (37) Could not open directory <path> for share <name>` and exits 37 for one that is missing or not a directory. Every server that serves files reaches a share, so `curl http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<name>/<file>` and `curl ftp://127.0.0.1:<port>/<name>/<file>` read the same file, and a share hides a root entry of its name. The same rules as the root apply inside it: `--allow-uploads`, `--list-directories`, `--follow-symlinks` and `--serve-dot-files`, a `..` stopping at the share, and a `.surl` folder hidden. `readonly` refuses every write in the share whatever `--allow-uploads` says, and `protocols=` limits it to the schemes named, each admitting its TLS twin, so `protocols=ftp` also serves `ftps`; to any other scheme the share is missing. Starts exit 2 for a share whose name is not one path segment or is `.surl`, and for a path that is, lies in or holds `--directory`'s path or another share's.",
            "`--share-access <share>=<member>:<r|rw>,...` says who may use a share, and may be given again for more shares. A member is an account name from `--user` or `--user-file`, or `@` and a group name from `--group`; `r` reads and lists, and `rw` also uploads, renames and deletes, though a write still needs `--allow-uploads` and a share without `readonly`. A share with a `--share-access` is closed to every account it does not name and to anonymous callers (TFTP, Gopher, an unsigned object-storage request, and any login `--allow-anonymous` accepted): such a share is missing to them, as one `protocols=` excludes is, and a write an `r` caller makes is refused as an upload is when `--allow-uploads` is off. A share no `--share-access` names stays open as the root is. The member `@everyone` grants every caller, anonymous ones included, which widens access, and is warned at start. A member naming no account or group exits 2 at start.",
            "`--bucket <name>=<path>` maps a bucket name of the object-storage servers (see the s3 topic) onto the content-store path `<path>`, such as `/media/photos` or `/team/photos` inside the share `team`; the original file servers never see bucket names. A share, a bucket or a `--share-access` share given twice (`share <name> is given twice`, `bucket <name> is given twice`), a bucket named as a share (`bucket <name> is also a --share`) and a `--share-access` naming no `--share` (`share <name> names no --share`) exit 2 as the command line is read, and no such refusal shows a path. With `-v`, surl notes `* Share <name>: <path>` and `* Bucket <name>: <path>` for each at start.",
        ],
