        ["http"] =
        [
            "The HTTP server answers GET and HEAD for files in the served root over HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1, one request after another on a persistent connection. A file is answered 200 OK with `Content-Length` and the `Content-Type` its extension names (`text/html` for `.html`, `image/png` for `.png`, ADR-0142), else `application/octet-stream`, then its bytes for GET.",
            "A GET with one `Range` (`curl -r 0-9`) is answered 206 Partial Content with `Content-Range: bytes <first>-<last>/<length>` and just those bytes, and a range that names no byte of the file 416 Range Not Satisfiable with `Content-Range: bytes */<length>` and an empty body. Several ranges, another unit and a malformed `Range` are ignored and the whole file is served 200, as it is when `If-Range` is not exactly the `Last-Modified` the answer carries.",
            "Under `--allow-uploads`, with a login (see the auth topic), a PUT (`curl -T <local file> -u user:password`) stores its body at the path, kept only once all of it has arrived: 201 Created when no file was there, 204 No Content when one was replaced, 403 Forbidden where the served root does not permit the file, 409 Conflict for a directory or a missing parent directory, and 413 past `--max-filesize` with nothing stored.",
            "On `http://` it also answers HTTP/2 started with prior knowledge (`curl --http2-prior-knowledge http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>`): a connection that opens with the HTTP/2 preface gets the same answers as HTTP/1.1, each request on its own stream, up to 100 streams at once, within the client's flow-control windows. A header block longer than `--max-message` or not complete within `--head-timeout` ends the connection with GOAWAY ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM, a stream past the 100 is refused with RST_STREAM REFUSED_STREAM, a malformed request is reset with RST_STREAM PROTOCOL_ERROR, and `--idle-timeout`, `--max-time` and shutdown end the connection with GOAWAY NO_ERROR. It also answers the `Upgrade: h2c` route (`curl --http2 http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>`): an HTTP/1.1 request with no body, one `HTTP2-Settings` that decodes, and both named in `Connection` gets `101 Switching Protocols` and its answer as HTTP/2 stream 1; a request with a body or a malformed `HTTP2-Settings` ignores the upgrade and is answered HTTP/1.1.",
            "A missing path, a refused path and a directory are answered 404 Not Found with an empty body. POST, PUT, DELETE, CONNECT, OPTIONS, TRACE and PATCH are answered 405 Method Not Allowed with `Allow: GET, HEAD`; under `--allow-uploads` PUT is served as above and the others' 405 says `Allow: GET, HEAD, PUT`, and any other method 501 Not Implemented. Every response names the server `Server: surl`, with no version.",
            "`https` is the same server over TLS from the first byte, so an `https` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic). It offers the ALPN IDs `h2` then `http/1.1`, and a connection that agrees `h2` is answered HTTP/2 as above, which curl asks for by default when it has HTTP/2 support (`curl -k https://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>`); one that agrees `http/1.1` (`curl --http1.1`) or no ALPN is answered HTTP/1.x. `Upgrade: h2c` over `https://` is ignored. NTLM and Negotiate are bound to the connection, which HTTP/2 does not carry: an HTTP/2 stream whose `authorization` names either, or whose `401` would offer only them, is reset `HTTP_1_1_REQUIRED` and curl retries the request over HTTP/1.1, where the login goes ahead.",
            "`--http3` also serves HTTP/3 over QUIC on UDP for every `https` listen URL, at the same address and port as its TCP listener (`curl -k --http3-only https://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>`), and adds `Alt-Svc: h3=\":<port>\"; ma=86400` to every answer the HTTP server gives over HTTP/1.x and HTTP/2 there, which curl acts on with `curl --alt-svc <file>`. It is off by default, and the status line is unchanged: one `Listening on https://<host>:<port>/` names the port both transports share; with `-v` surl writes `* Serving HTTP/3 over QUIC on UDP port <port>`. `--http3` with no `https` listen URL or with `--tls-max` below 1.3 (QUIC is TLS 1.3 only) exits 2. With `--cacert`, the QUIC handshake requires a client certificate that chains to those anchors, as the TCP listener does (`curl --http3-only --cacert <server-ca> --cert <client.pem> --key <client.key> https://localhost:<port>/<file>`); without one the handshake fails.",
            "Logins follow the auth topic: with an account over `http://`, log in with Digest (`curl --digest -u user:password`), since Basic and Bearer are refused unchecked there unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given.",
        ],
        ["limits"] =
        [
            "Each limit is 0 for no limit:",
            "- `--max-connections <number>`: connections at once, all listeners together; default 1024.",
            "- `--max-connections-per-address <number>`: connections at once from one address; default 100.",
            "- `--idle-timeout <seconds>`: an exchange idle this long is closed; default 120.",
            "- `-m, --max-time <seconds>`: the longest one exchange may take; default 3600.",
            "- `--head-timeout <seconds>`: the time a client has to send a request head, command line or first packet; default 30.",
            "- `--max-request-head <bytes>`: the largest HTTP request head; default 100k.",
            "- `--max-line <bytes>`: the longest command line; default 8192.",
            "- `--max-message <bytes>`: the largest framed message, such as an MQTT packet; default 1M.",
            "- `--max-filesize <bytes>`: the largest upload; default 100M.",
            "A size may end in k, M, G, T or P, each 1024 times the one before. A limit reached ends only the connection or exchange that reached it, answered in its protocol's own words where it has them; it never ends surl.",
