        ["mqtt5"] =
        [
            "The MQTT 5.0 broker answers `mqtt5` (TCP), `mqtt5s` (TLS from the first byte) and `mqtt5+ws` (MQTT over WebSocket, path `/mqtt`, subprotocol `mqtt`) listen URLs. It is an extended server (see the extended topic): it is judged by the MQTT Version 5.0 specification (https://docs.oasis-open.org/mqtt/mqtt/v5.0/mqtt-v5.0.html), not by upstream curl, which has no `mqtt5` scheme. `mqtt` stays the MQTT 3.1.1 server and is unchanged.",
            "It shares the one message bus the `mqtt`, NATS, STOMP, AMQP and Kafka servers use, so a message retained over `mqtt://` is delivered to an `mqtt5://` subscriber in the same run, and the reverse; retained messages persist where the bus does (`<path>/.surl/bus/` with `--directory`). Only session state is the broker's.",
            "Every CONNECT needs a login unless `--allow-anonymous` is given (see the auth topic). `mqtt5s` needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic). `mqtt5` defaults to port 1883, `mqtt5s` to 8883 and `mqtt5+ws` to 80.",
        ],
        ["messages"] =
        [
            "The messaging servers (MQTT, NATS and the other brokers) share one message bus, so a message published over one protocol reaches a matching subscriber of another. Without any option here the bus maps each protocol's names to topics by its default rules, every logged-in caller may publish and subscribe, and no topic or queue exists before something uses it.",
            "`--topic <filter>[;retain][;qos=<0|1|2>]` declares a topic filter, and may be given again. `retain` keeps every publish on a matching topic as the topic's retained message, whichever protocol published it; `qos=<n>` carries a matching publish at QoS `n` at most, `n` exactly `0`, `1` or `2` (anything else exits 2 with `is badly used here`). A filter without wildcards exists from start, so a Kafka metadata request lists it. Starts exit 2 for a filter that is not a valid topic filter (`topic <filter> is not a valid topic filter`).",
            "`--queue <name>[;durable]` creates a named queue at start that no client may delete; `durable` keeps it and its messages under `--directory`'s `.surl/bus`, and without it the queue is empty at every start. More than 1000 queues exit 2 at start.",
            "`--topic-map <scheme>:<name>=<scheme>:<name>` makes the left side's name, on its scheme, mean the topic the right side's name has on its scheme: `amqp:orders=mqtt:shop/orders` joins AMQP's routing key `orders` to MQTT's `shop/orders`, both ways. A scheme no surl server speaks exits 2 as the line is read (`unknown scheme <scheme>`); one that is not a messaging scheme, a name not valid on its scheme (`not a valid name for <scheme>`) and a name mapped twice on a scheme and its TLS twin exit 2 at start.",
            "`--topic-access <filter>=<member>:<pub|sub|pubsub>,...` says who may publish or subscribe, and may be given again. A member is an account name from `--user` or `--user-file`, or `@` and a group name from `--group`. Once one is given every publish and subscribe needs a grant, and an account with none, like every anonymous caller, is refused; a queue is named as the filter `$queue/<name>`. The member `@everyone` grants every caller, anonymous ones included, which widens access and is warned at start. A member naming no account or group exits 2 at start.",
            "A topic, a queue, a topic-map source (`topic map <scheme>:<name> is given twice`) or a `--topic-access` filter given twice exits 2 as the command line is read (`topic <filter> is given twice`, `queue <name> is given twice`). With `-v`, surl notes `* Topic <filter>`, `* Queue <name>`, `* Topic map <from> = <to>` and `* Topic access: <n> grants` at start.",
