        ["ldap"] =
        [
            "The LDAP server (LDAPv3, RFC 4511) answers the search upstream curl encodes in an `ldap://` URL - base DN, attributes, scope and filter - from one read-only directory: `curl -u alice:secret 'ldap://127.0.0.1:<port>/dc=example,dc=com?cn,mail?sub?(objectClass=person)'` prints the matching entries. A URL with no base DN, `ldap://127.0.0.1:<port>/`, reads the root DSE. Add, delete, modify and rename are refused `unwillingToPerform`.",
            "Without `--directory` the directory is empty; with it, it is read once at start from `<path>/.surl/ldap/directory.ldif`, RFC 2849 LDIF content records in UTF-8, and a missing file is an empty directory. A file that cannot be read or is not LDIF the directory can hold writes `surl: (37) Could not read <file>: line <n>: <what>` and exits 37 before any listener binds.",
            "Every search but the root DSE's needs a successful bind on the connection unless `--allow-anonymous` is given, and is answered `insufficientAccessRights` without one. A simple bind (`curl -u user:password`) is checked against `--user` and `--user-file`, the bind name being the account name or the value of a DN's leading `cn` or `uid`; over `ldap://` without StartTLS it is refused `confidentialityRequired` unchecked unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given (curl exits 38). The Windows curl's other binds, from its NTLM, Negotiate and Digest options, are NTLM (accepted with `--auth ntlm` or `--auth negotiate`) and DIGEST-MD5 (`--auth digest-md5`), each needing an account and each followed by the security layer it negotiates. With `--keytab` and `--auth negotiate`, the Windows curl's Negotiate bind to `ldap://localhost:<port>` (a host name, not `127.0.0.1`) is answered with Kerberos inside `GSS-SPNEGO`: the ticket is for the service principal `ldap/<the machine's host name>:<port>`, with the port, which the keytab must hold a key for, its client principal must be an account in `--user-file` (or `--allow-anonymous` given), and every later message is sealed in RFC 4121 wrap tokens; without `--keytab` the Negotiate bind is answered with NTLM. The SASL mechanisms `--auth` accepts are listed in the root DSE.",
            "With `--cert` or `--self-signed`, `ldap://` offers StartTLS; without a certificate it is refused `protocolError`. `ldaps` is the same server over TLS from the first byte, so an `ldaps` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic). The Windows curl checks the server's certificate itself and ignores `-k`, so it cannot use `ldaps` against a certificate Windows does not trust and exits 38.",
            "A message longer than `--max-message` is answered with the Notice of Disconnection and closed, a connection that sends no message within `--head-timeout` is closed, and one search returns at most 10000 entries, then `sizeLimitExceeded`.",
        ],
        ["proxy"] =
        [
            "`--proxy <listen URL>` (`-x`), repeatable, makes one listener a proxy instead of a server: `surl --proxy http://127.0.0.1:3128` is an HTTP proxy and `surl --proxy https://127.0.0.1:3129 --cert <file> --key <file>` an HTTPS proxy, the one curl reaches with `-x https://...`. A bare `http://` listen URL stays the HTTP server. The default ports are curl's own, 1080 for `http://` and 443 for `https://`, and `--proxy` also takes the four SOCKS schemes (see the socks topic); any other scheme exits 1 (`Protocol \"<scheme>\" not supported`). A proxy listen URL needs no other listen URL, and it cannot be combined with `--unix-socket` or `--abstract-unix-socket` (exit 2).",
            "The proxy forwards the absolute-form requests curl sends it (`curl -x http://127.0.0.1:<port>/ http://<origin>/`) and opens a tunnel for `CONNECT` (`curl -p -x ...`, and every https URL through `-x`). By default it connects only to loopback origins, and never to its own listener. `--proxy-allow-origin <address[/prefix]>`, repeatable, adds an IPv4 or IPv6 address or range (`10.0.0.0/8`; no prefix is the one address; host names are refused); `--proxy-allow-origin '*'` admits every origin and writes `surl: warning: --proxy-allow-origin: the proxy connects to every origin address, not only loopback` from the info level up.",
            "Without `-U` or `--proxy-user-file` the proxy asks for no login. `-U <user:password>` (`--proxy-user`), repeatable, and `--proxy-user-file <file>` (the format of `--user-file`, see the auth topic) are the proxy's accounts alone, separate from `--user` accounts: a request then needs a `Proxy-Authorization` header, and is otherwise answered `407` with `Proxy-Authenticate`. `--proxy-auth <methods>` (the words of `--auth`, see the auth topic) sets the methods the proxy accepts and offers in its `407`, `digest,basic` by default, and is separate from `--auth`: `--proxy-auth basic,ntlm` adds NTLM for the proxy alone, and surl writes `surl: warning: --proxy-auth: accepted proxy methods are basic, ntlm` from the info level up. `--allow-anonymous` and `--allow-plaintext-auth` loosen the proxy's login as they do every login. An unreadable proxy user file exits 37 and a malformed one exits 2.",
            "The ADR-0006 limits apply: `--max-connections` and `--max-connections-per-address` count curl's connections, `--idle-timeout` and `--max-time` cover forwarding and tunnels, `--head-timeout` bounds curl's request head (`408`) and the origin's connect and response head (`504`), `--max-request-head` bounds curl's request head (`431`) and the origin's response head (`502`), and `--max-filesize` bounds a forwarded request body (`413`). A tunnel's bytes are bounded only by the time limits. An `https` proxy needs `--cert` and `--key`, or `--self-signed` for a test (see the tls topic).",
        ],
        ["rtsp"] =
        [
            "The RTSP server (RFC 2326) answers the ten requests upstream libcurl's `CURLOPT_RTSP_REQUEST` sends: `OPTIONS`, `DESCRIBE`, `ANNOUNCE`, `SETUP`, `PLAY`, `PAUSE`, `TEARDOWN`, `GET_PARAMETER`, `SET_PARAMETER` and `RECORD`. The `curl` tool has no option that chooses the request and sends only `OPTIONS *`: `curl rtsp://127.0.0.1:<port>/` is answered `RTSP/1.0 200 OK` with a `Public` field naming all ten, and exits 0.",
            "Every file in the served root is a presentation of one stream whose RTP payloads are the file's bytes, in order. `DESCRIBE` answers its SDP description (`application/sdp`, one `m=application` stream of payload type 96), and `PLAY` streams it in RTP packets of 1400 payload bytes, then an RTCP sender report and `BYE`. A path that is missing, hidden or a directory is `404 Not Found`; RTSP has no listings.",
            "Only interleaved TCP transport is served: `SETUP` takes the first unicast `RTP/AVP/TCP` alternative of its `Transport` field and answers `461 Unsupported Transport` when there is none, so RTP over UDP and multicast are refused. A connection holds at most one session, ended by `TEARDOWN`, by the connection ending, or by 60 seconds without a request while it neither plays nor records.",
            "`ANNOUNCE <path>` stores its body as `<path>.sdp`, and `SETUP` with `mode=record` then `RECORD` store the RTP payloads the client sends at `<path>`, committed by `TEARDOWN` and deleted when the session ends any other way. Both are uploads: refused `403 Forbidden` without `--allow-uploads`, and a body past `--max-filesize` is answered `413` or the recording deleted.",
            "Requests are judged as HTTP requests are (see the auth topic): with no account configured a read needs no login; once any account is configured every request needs one, `OPTIONS` included, and an upload always needs one unless `--allow-anonymous` is given. A `401` offers Digest, which curl answers on the same connection (`curl --digest -u user:password`); Basic and Bearer are refused `403 Forbidden` unchecked unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given. curl has no `rtsps`, so RTSP is always plain text.",
            "A request head is bounded by `--max-request-head` and `--head-timeout`, answered `431` and `408` and then closed, as the HTTP server answers them, and a connection past `--max-connections` is answered `RTSP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable`.",
        ],
        ["s3"] =
        [
            "The S3 server answers Amazon S3 REST requests over HTTP/1.1 on the one served root: each top-level folder is a bucket and the path under it is the object key, so a file another protocol wrote is an object and an object PutObject stored is a file. It is an extended server (see the extended topic): it is judged by the Amazon S3 API Reference, not by upstream curl, which has no `s3` scheme and reaches it as `https://`.",
            "Requests are addressed path style (`/<bucket>/<key>`) or virtual-hosted style (`Host: <bucket>.<domain>`, where the domain is the listen URL's host when it is a DNS name, `localhost`, `s3.amazonaws.com` or `s3.<region>.amazonaws.com`). It serves GetObject and HeadObject, with one `Range` and the conditional fields; PutObject and DeleteObject, which need `--allow-uploads`; HeadBucket; and ListObjectsV2 (`GET /<bucket>?list-type=2`) and ListBuckets (`GET /`), which need `--list-directories`. Without the option each is `403 AccessDenied`, and every other request is `501 NotImplemented`. DeleteObject answers `204` whether or not the key existed, and removes a folder key (`x/`) only when the folder is empty. Every error carries S3's XML `Error` body.",
            "Every request must be signed with AWS Signature Version 4 for the service `s3` by an account (`--user`, `--user-file`): the user name is the access key ID and the password the secret access key. An unsigned request is `403 AccessDenied`, an unknown key `403 InvalidAccessKeyId`, a bad signature `403 SignatureDoesNotMatch` and a date more than 15 minutes off `403 RequestTimeTooSkewed`. With `--allow-anonymous` unsigned reads are served and unsigned writes are still refused. `--auth` does not apply to `s3`, and no `401` or `WWW-Authenticate` is ever sent.",
            "`s3` is TLS from the first byte, as every S3 endpoint is, so an `s3` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic); ALPN offers `http/1.1` only. `--s3-plaintext` serves every `s3` listen URL as plain HTTP/1.1 instead, with no certificate, for a client pointed at an `http://` custom endpoint (`AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://...`); requests are still signed and checked, and SigV4 sends no secret, so `--allow-plaintext-auth` is not needed. A start that gives it writes `surl: warning: --s3-plaintext: s3 is served as plain HTTP, without TLS` from the info level up. A request head is bounded by `--max-request-head` (answered `400 RequestHeaderSectionTooLarge`) and `--head-timeout`, and an upload by `--max-filesize` (`400 EntityTooLarge`).",
        ],
        ["socketio"] =
        [
            "The Socket.IO server answers Socket.IO clients for `socketio` and `socketios` listen URLs. It is an extended server (see the extended topic): it is judged by the Engine.IO protocol, 4th version, and the Socket.IO protocol, 5th version, not by upstream curl, which has no `socketio` scheme. One server answers both schemes, so its sessions and broadcasts span every Socket.IO listen URL of the run.",
            "A client reaches it at the path `/socket.io/` with `EIO=4`, over HTTP long-polling or a WebSocket upgrade; any other path is `404`. It serves the main namespace `/` only, because a listen URL has no path to name another.",
            "A session's first Socket.IO packet must be a CONNECT, and its login is judged by the accounts allowed on `socketio` (`--user`, `--user-file`). Without an account a login is refused unless `--allow-anonymous` is given, and a password over plain `socketio` is refused unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given.",
            "`socketio` is plain HTTP. `socketios` is the same server inside TLS from the first byte, so a `socketios` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic).",
        ],
        ["socks"] =
        [
            "The SOCKS server answers the proxy requests curl sends with `--socks4`, `--socks4a`, `--socks5`, `--socks5-hostname` and `-x`/`--proxy` given a `socks4://`, `socks4a://`, `socks5://` or `socks5h://` URL. Give the listen URL positionally or with `--proxy`: `surl socks5://127.0.0.1:1080` and `surl --proxy socks4://127.0.0.1:1080` are the same server. The default port is 1080, as curl's is for every SOCKS scheme.",
            "`--unix-socket <path>` (or `--abstract-unix-socket <name>`) serves the one SOCKS listen URL on a Unix domain socket instead of TCP: `surl --unix-socket /tmp/surl-socks.sock socks5h://localhost` is the server curl reaches with `--proxy socks5h://localhost/tmp/surl-socks.sock` or `--socks5 localhost/tmp/surl-socks.sock`. Give the listen URL positionally there: a `--proxy` listen URL cannot be combined with a Unix socket (exit 2). SOCKS4 serves on a socket file too.",
            "The scheme says which versions a listener serves: `socks4` and `socks4a` serve SOCKS4 and SOCKS4a, and answer a SOCKS5 greeting `05 FF`; `socks5` and `socks5h` serve SOCKS5 with IPv4, IPv6 and domain-name addresses, and answer a SOCKS4 request `00 5B`. Either way curl exits 97 with a clear refusal instead of hanging. A `socks4` and a `socks5` listener cannot share a port.",
            "Only `CONNECT` is served: `BIND` and `UDP ASSOCIATE` are refused, since curl sends neither. The server connects each request itself and relays the bytes both ways. Like the HTTP proxy it connects only to loopback origins, and never to its own listener, unless `--proxy-allow-origin <address[/prefix]>` widens that (see the proxy topic).",
            "Without `-U` or `--proxy-user-file` the server asks for no login. `-U <user:password>` and `--proxy-user-file <file>` name the accounts of every proxy listener, SOCKS included, and SOCKS5 then requires RFC 1929 username and password; SOCKS4 has no password, so a SOCKS4 request is refused when accounts exist, unless `--allow-anonymous`. The password travels in clear, so a login is refused unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given. `--proxy-auth` does not apply to SOCKS.",
            "`--head-timeout` bounds the whole handshake and the origin's connect; a SOCKS4 user ID or SOCKS4a host name longer than 255 bytes is refused. The listen URL cannot have a path, query or user name (exit 2). `--max-connections`, `--max-connections-per-address`, `--idle-timeout` and `--max-time` apply as to every server.",
        ],
        ["webtransport"] =
        [
            "The WebTransport server answers WebTransport clients for `webtransport` listen URLs, over QUIC and HTTP/3 on UDP. It is an extended server (see the extended topic): it is judged by the WebTransport over HTTP/3 draft, draft-ietf-webtrans-http3-16 (the WTH3 draft), with RFC 9220, RFC 9297 and RFC 9221, not by upstream curl, which has no `webtransport` scheme. The default port is 443.",
            "A client opens a session with an extended `CONNECT` whose `:protocol` is `webtransport-h3`; every path accepts a session, because a listen URL has no path to name one, and every other request is answered `405`. The server echoes the streams and datagrams a session carries, applies WTH3 flow control, and ends a session with `WT_CLOSE_SESSION`.",
            "Each `CONNECT` login is judged by the accounts allowed on `webtransport` (`--user`, `--user-file`), as an HTTP request is: without an account a login is refused unless `--allow-anonymous` is given.",
            "QUIC is always TLS 1.3, so a `webtransport` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic); ALPN offers `h3`. It binds UDP only, and `--idle-timeout` is the QUIC `max_idle_timeout`.",
        ],
        ["xmpp"] =
        [
            "The XMPP server answers XMPP clients (RFC 6120) for `xmpp` listen URLs, and for `xmpps` ones, XMPP inside TLS from the first byte (XEP-0368). It is an extended server (see the extended topic): it is judged by RFC 6120, not by upstream curl, which has no `xmpp` scheme. The default ports are 5222 for `xmpp` and 5223 for `xmpps`.",
            "A client opens a stream to the listen URL's host or to a `--mail-domain`, logs in with SASL, binds a resource and exchanges stanzas: messages are routed between the streams one run has bound, and an IQ is answered with service discovery, ping and an empty roster. Every refusal is a stream error, then the close of the stream.",
            "With `--cert` or `--self-signed` an `xmpp` stream offers STARTTLS, and requires it before a login unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given (see the tls topic). Without a certificate `xmpp` never offers TLS. `xmpps` needs `--cert` or `--self-signed`, and offers the ALPN protocol `xmpp-client`.",
            "A login is judged by the accounts allowed on `xmpp` (`--user`, `--user-file`) through the SASL mechanisms `--auth` leaves on, and without an account only `--allow-anonymous` lets a client in (see the auth topic).",
        ],
        ["security"] =
        [
            "Every option here widens what a peer may do, and each is off by default. Seven are deployment choices that write no warning: `--allow-uploads`, `--list-directories`, `--follow-symlinks`, `--serve-dot-files`, `--allow-zone-transfer`, `--tlsv1.0` and `--tlsv1.1`; leave each off unless it is needed.",
            "`--allow-weak-ssh-algorithms` also offers SSH algorithms built on SHA-1, MD5, CBC, RC4, 3DES and 1024-bit Diffie-Hellman. It is for peers that support nothing stronger; a start that gives it writes `surl: warning: --allow-weak-ssh-algorithms: SHA-1, MD5, CBC, RC4, 3DES and 1024-bit Diffie-Hellman SSH algorithms are offered` from the info level up, and it also lets a `--hostkey` file hold an RSA key shorter than 2048 bits or a DSA key.",
            "`--proxy-allow-origin <address[/prefix]>` lets the proxy (see the proxy topic) connect to the addresses it names besides loopback; `--proxy-allow-origin '*'` lets it connect to every origin, and writes `surl: warning: --proxy-allow-origin: the proxy connects to every origin address, not only loopback` from the info level up.",
            "The other nine, `--allow-anonymous`, `--allow-plaintext-auth`, `--auth`, `--control`, `--control-token-file`, `--fault`, `--s3-plaintext`, `--self-signed` and `--throwaway-hostkey`, loosen a secure default and are for tests only (see the testing topic).",
            "Before running surl where others can reach it: bind a specific address rather than `0.0.0.0` or `[::]`; give `--cert` and `--key` for secure schemes, never `--self-signed`; configure accounts with `--user-file`; review the limits; start with no loosening option, and treat any `surl: warning:` line at start as one left on.",
        ],
        ["smb"] =
        [
            "The SMB server speaks SMB version 1 (`NT LM 0.12`), the only dialect upstream curl uses for `smb://` and `smbs://` URLs. Each top-level directory of the served root is a share, named by the URL's first path segment: `curl -u user:password smb://127.0.0.1:<port>/<share>/<file>` downloads `<share>/<file>`, and `curl -T <local file> -u user:password smb://127.0.0.1:<port>/<share>/<file>` uploads it, which needs `--allow-uploads` and replaces a file already there. A missing share or file, a directory, or a name the content rules hide makes curl exit 78; an upload without `--allow-uploads` exits 9, and one past `--max-filesize` exits 25 with the partial file deleted.",
            "curl logs in with NTLMv1, so a session setup is refused unless `--auth` names `ntlmv1` and the user and password match an account (`-u` or `--user-file`), or `--allow-anonymous` is given, when every session setup is accepted as a guest. The domain curl sends is ignored. A refused session setup ends the connection and curl exits 67; one refused because `--auth` does not name `ntlmv1` writes `SMB session setup refused: ntlmv1 is not in --auth`. NTLMv1 sends no password, so no `--allow-plaintext-auth` is needed, but its responses are open to offline cracking: `smbs` keeps them off the wire.",
            "A message longer than `--max-message` (at most 131071 bytes, SMB's 17-bit length) is answered with an error and the session goes on; a client that sends no negotiate within `--head-timeout` is disconnected.",
            "`smbs` is the same server over TLS from the first byte, so an `smbs` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic). Both schemes default to port 445.",
        ],
        ["smtp"] =
        [
            "The SMTP server (RFC 5321) receives the mail upstream curl sends. `curl --mail-from <address> --mail-rcpt <address> -T <file> smtp://127.0.0.1:<port>/<domain>` sends `<file>` as one message, which is stored in the `INBOX` of each recipient whose local part names an account; a recipient that names no account is answered as one that does, and its copy is discarded. `curl smtp://127.0.0.1:<port>/` with nothing to send is answered with the `HELP` reply.",
            "`MAIL` needs a login unless `--allow-anonymous` is given, when every recipient's copy goes to one anonymous `INBOX`. `curl -u user:password` logs in with `AUTH` and a SASL mechanism `--auth` accepts (see the auth topic); over `smtp://` without STARTTLS, PLAIN and LOGIN are not offered unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given, and CRAM-MD5 is.",
            "With `--cert` or `--self-signed`, `smtp://` offers STARTTLS (`curl --ssl-reqd`); without a certificate STARTTLS is answered 454. `smtps` is the same server over TLS from the first byte, so an `smtps` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic).",
            "Without `--directory` the mail store lives in memory only; with it, it is kept in `<path>/.surl/mail` and loaded at start, and an index or message file that cannot be read or does not parse exits 37. A message past `--max-filesize`, counted with the `Return-Path` and `Received` fields surl adds, is answered 552 and not stored.",
        ],
        ["ssh"] =
        [
            "The SSH server (SSH 2.0, RFC 4251 to RFC 4254) answers `scp` and `sftp` listen URLs alike: one server, whose SCP command and SFTP subsystem serve the one served root. `curl -u user:password -k sftp://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>` and `scp://` read `<file>`; `curl -T <local file>` writes it, which needs `--allow-uploads`; `curl -Q` sends SFTP commands such as `rm`, `rename` and `mkdir`, and an SFTP directory is listed only with `--list-directories`.",
            "An `scp` or `sftp` listen URL needs a host key: `--hostkey <file>` (an OpenSSH, PKCS #8, PKCS #1 or SEC 1 private key; RSA of at least 2048 bits, ECDSA or Ed25519; one per key type; `--pass` for an encrypted one), or `--throwaway-hostkey` for a throwaway RSA key made at start, for tests only. Without either, surl writes `surl: (2) <url> needs a host key: give --hostkey <file>, or --throwaway-hostkey for a throwaway one` and exits 2. A `--hostkey` file that cannot be read exits 37, and one surl cannot use exits 2, naming the file.",
            "curl checks the host key: with `-v`, surl writes `* Serving SSH host key <key type>, --hostpubsha256 <base64> --hostpubmd5 <hex>` for each key before the Listening on lines, and a test passes `--hostpubsha256 <base64>` to curl, or `-k` to skip the check.",
            "`--hostcert <file>`, repeatable, names an OpenSSH host certificate for one of the `--hostkey` keys: the one-line `*-cert.pub` file `ssh-keygen -s <ca> -h` writes for an RSA, ECDSA or Ed25519 key, at most one per certificate type. The key is then also offered under each of its algorithms with `-cert-v01@openssh.com` added, just before the plain one (`rsa-sha2-512-cert-v01@openssh.com`, `ssh-ed25519-cert-v01@openssh.com`, and `ssh-rsa-cert-v01@openssh.com` only with `--allow-weak-ssh-algorithms`), and a client that picks one receives the certificate as the host key. surl judges neither the certificate's validity period nor its CA's signature: the client does. A file that cannot be read writes `surl: (37) Could not read host certificate <file>` and exits 37; one that is not a host certificate (a user certificate included) writes `surl: (2) Host certificate <file>: not an OpenSSH host certificate`, one whose key is no `--hostkey` key `surl: (2) Host certificate <file>: certifies no --hostkey key`, and a second of one type `surl: (2) Host certificate <file>: a <type> host certificate is already given by <file>`, each exiting 2.",
            "Every login needs an account unless `--allow-anonymous` is given: a password or keyboard-interactive login is checked against `--user` and `--user-file`, and a public-key login against `--authorized-keys` (see the auth topic). Six refused attempts end the connection.",
            "`--head-timeout` covers everything from the connection to the login, `--max-message` bounds one SSH packet, and `--max-filesize` one upload.",
            "`--ssh-ciphers <names>` and `--ssh-macs <names>` narrow the offer to the comma-separated ciphers or MACs named, in the order named, as sshd's `Ciphers` and `MACs` do, so a test can make one algorithm the only one curl can agree to; the last one given wins. "
            + "The ciphers are `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`, `aes256-gcm@openssh.com` and `aes128-gcm@openssh.com` (where the machine has AES-GCM), `aes256-ctr`, `aes192-ctr` and `aes128-ctr`, and with `--allow-weak-ssh-algorithms` also `aes256-cbc`, `rijndael-cbc@lysator.liu.se`, `aes192-cbc`, `aes128-cbc`, `3des-cbc`, `arcfour128`, `arcfour`, `blowfish-cbc` and `cast128-cbc`. "
            + "The MACs are `hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com`, `hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com`, `hmac-sha2-256` and `hmac-sha2-512`, and with `--allow-weak-ssh-algorithms` also `hmac-sha1-etm@openssh.com`, `hmac-sha1`, `hmac-sha1-96`, `hmac-md5`, `hmac-md5-96`, `hmac-ripemd160` and `hmac-ripemd160@openssh.com`. "
            + "Names are exact, in lower case as listed. Any other name writes `surl: (2) --ssh-ciphers: surl does not offer the SSH cipher <name>` (or `--ssh-macs: surl does not offer the SSH MAC <name>`), and a weak one without `--allow-weak-ssh-algorithms` writes `surl: (2) --ssh-ciphers: <name> needs --allow-weak-ssh-algorithms`; each exits 2.",
        ],
        ["stomp"] =
        [
            "The STOMP broker answers STOMP 1.2 (CONNECT or STOMP, SEND, SUBSCRIBE, UNSUBSCRIBE, ACK, NACK, BEGIN, COMMIT, ABORT and DISCONNECT) for `stomp` and `stomps` listen URLs. It is an extended server (see the extended topic): it is judged by the STOMP 1.2 specification (https://stomp.github.io/stomp-specification-1.2.html), not by upstream curl, which has no `stomp` scheme.",
            "Its destinations are those of the one message bus the MQTT and NATS servers also use, so a message sent over `stomp://` reaches a matching MQTT subscriber in the same run, and the reverse.",
            "Every CONNECT needs a login unless `--allow-anonymous` is given: `login` and `passcode` log in as that account (`--user`, `--user-file`). A refused login is answered with an ERROR frame and closed. A password over `stomp://` is refused unchecked unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given (see the auth topic).",
            "`stomps` is TLS from the first byte, so a `stomps` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic). `stomp` defaults to port 61613 and `stomps` to 61614. The server proposes heart-beats from `--idle-timeout`. A `stomps` connection past `--max-connections` is closed without an ERROR frame.",
        ],
        ["surl"] =
        [
            "`-h, --help <subject>`, `-M, --manual` and `-V, --version` each end the reading of the command line, write their text to stdout and exit 0, whatever else is on it. The first of them read wins; an error read before it still refuses the command line.",
            "`surl --help` writes the short list; `surl --help <subject>` writes a category (`surl --help category` lists them), `all` for every option, or one option's page. `surl --manual` writes the whole manual, and `surl --version` the version and the schemes this build serves.",
            "A command line with no listen URL writes `surl: (2) no URL specified` and the `try` line to stderr and exits 2.",
            "`-K, --config <file>` reads options from a file in curl's config-file syntax, in place: the file's options act as if they stood where the `-K` stands, so a later option overrides one from the file and the file overrides an earlier one; it may be given again, and `-K -` reads standard input, once. No file is read unless named. "
            + "Each line holds one option and at most its value: `user = \"ann:pass word\"`, `--user \"ann:pass word\"` and `-u \"ann:pass word\"` alike; a name without dashes is separated from its value by spaces, tabs, `=` or `:`, a dashed one by spaces or tabs. "
            + "In double quotes `\\\\`, `\\\"`, `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\r` and `\\v` are escapes and a backslash before another character is dropped. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped, and `#` after a value starts a comment. "
            + "A listen URL is written `url = <listen URL>` (`--url <listen URL>` on the command line too); a URL alone on a line is an unknown option. `config = <file>` reads another file in place, at most 5 open at once. "
            + "Unlike curl, surl refuses text after a value, a missing closing quote, a file including one already being read and a second `-K -`, and counts every physical line. A refusal is prefixed by `<file>:<line>: ` (`<stdin>` for `-`) and exits 2, such as `surl: a.cfg:2: option --log-file: requires parameter`; a file that cannot be read writes `surl: (37) Could not read config file <file>` and exits 37. A file is at most 16 MiB, a line at most 10 MiB, in UTF-8. On Linux and macOS, each config file read, nested ones included, whose mode lets the group or other users read it is warned at every start with `surl: warning: <file> is readable by other users and may hold passwords` (`chmod 600` the file to silence it); standard input and Windows never are.",
        ],
        ["telnet"] =
        [
            "The TELNET server (RFC 854, RFC 855) negotiates options with the client, reports what the client tells it about its terminal and environment, then echoes each line back until the client sends `quit`.",
            "`curl telnet://127.0.0.1:<port>/` carries the session: what curl reads from stdin is sent, and what the server answers is written to stdout. `curl -t <option=value>` sets curl's side of the negotiation.",
        ],
        ["testing"] =
        [
            "`--allow-anonymous`, `--allow-plaintext-auth`, `--auth` and `--self-signed` each loosen a secure default so a test can reach surl without accounts, without TLS or without a certificate file. They are for tests only.",
            "Each writes a `surl: warning:` line on start, from the info level up, to the log (stderr unless `--log-file` is given); `--self-signed` writes its line only when it makes its certificate. What each one loosens, and why none is the default, follows the option table.",
            "`--s3-plaintext` serves `s3` as plain HTTP/1.1 instead of TLS, so an S3 client can test against an `http://` endpoint without a certificate, and writes `surl: warning: --s3-plaintext: s3 is served as plain HTTP, without TLS` on start (see the s3 topic).",
            "`--throwaway-hostkey` loosens the SSH host key the same way `--self-signed` loosens the certificate: surl makes a throwaway RSA key when it serves an `scp` or `sftp` listen URL and writes `surl: warning: --throwaway-hostkey: serving a throwaway SSH host key (--hostpubsha256 <base64>); clients must pin it or skip the check (curl -k)` (see the ssh topic).",
            "`--fault <kind>[;<key>=<value>]` (repeatable, at most 256) makes surl misbehave on purpose so a test can check how its client copes: `delay;ms=500;stage=command;path=/slow`, `drop;listener=smtp://127.0.0.1:2525;times=1`, `refuse-login;account=alice`, `error;scheme=smtp;command=RCPT;reply=451 4.3.0 try later` or `out-of-space;path=/uploads`. An `error` reply must fit its scheme's error shape (an HTTP status 400 to 599, an SMTP or FTP reply code 400 to 599 and text, IMAP `NO` or `BAD` and text, and so on), and an unknown kind or key, a key the kind does not take, a missing `ms` or `reply`, a number out of range or a reply that does not fit is refused, exit 2.",
            "`--random-seed <n>` (0 to 9223372036854775807, digits only; `random-seed = <n>` in a `-K` file) sets the seed for the non-secret identifiers a server draws, such as generated names that grant nothing, so a test can make them repeat; keys, nonces, challenges, salts and tokens are never drawn from it. It loosens nothing and writes no warning; without it nothing is seeded. Any other value writes `surl: option --random-seed: expected a proper numerical parameter` and exits 2.",
        ],
        ["tftp"] =
        [
            "The TFTP server (RFC 1350) listens on UDP and answers read and write requests with the `blksize`, `tsize` and `timeout` options (RFC 2347 to RFC 2349) upstream curl sends, from and into the served root.",
            "`curl tftp://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>` reads `<file>`; `curl -T <local file> tftp://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file>` writes it, which needs `--allow-uploads`. A missing or refused file is answered with TFTP ERROR 1, a write while uploads are off with ERROR 2, and a write past `--max-filesize` with ERROR 3.",
        ],
        ["tls"] =
        [
            "`https`, `ftps`, `gophers`, `imaps`, `ldaps`, `mqtts`, `pop3s`, `smbs`, `smtps`, `wss` and the extended `amqps`, `az`, `abfss`, `gs`, `kafkas`, `mqtt5s`, `s3`, `socketios`, `stomps` and `xmpps` start with a TLS handshake, so a listen URL of one of them needs a certificate: `--cert <file>` (PEM unless `--cert-type` says DER or P12), with `--key <file>` when the key is not in the `--cert` file and `--pass <phrase>` when the key needs one.",
            "Without `--cert` and without `--self-signed`, such a listen URL writes `surl: (58) <url> needs a certificate: give --cert <file>, or --self-signed for a throwaway one` and exits 58, and a `--cert` or `--key` file that cannot be used exits 58. `--key` or `--key-type` without `--cert`, `--pass` without `--cert` or `--hostkey`, `--key` with `--cert-type P12`, and `--self-signed` with `--cert` are refused, exit 2.",
            "`--self-signed` is for tests: surl makes a throwaway certificate at start and writes `surl: warning: --self-signed: serving a throwaway certificate; clients must skip verification (curl -k)`.",
            "surl accepts TLS 1.2 and 1.3 by default. `--tlsv1.0`, `--tlsv1.1`, `--tlsv1.2` and `--tlsv1.3` set the lowest version accepted, and `--tls-max <version>` the highest.",
            "`--cacert <file>` names the CA certificates for client certificates; a file that does not exist exits 2, and one that cannot be read exits 77.",
            "TLS-SRP (RFC 5054) authenticates with a password instead of a certificate, for `curl --tlsauthtype SRP --tlsuser <name> --tlspassword <password>`. `--tlsauthtype SRP` makes every TLS listen URL, and every STARTTLS upgrade, serve TLS 1.2 with the unsigned SRP suites and no certificate, so no `--cert` or `--self-signed` is needed; curl then needs `-k`, and without it exits 60. `--tlsuser` with `--tlspassword` gives one account, and implies `--tlsauthtype SRP` as in curl; `--srp-verifiers <file>` with `--srp-groups <file>` reads GnuTLS `tpasswd` and `tpasswd.conf` files, the ones gnutls-serv reads. A wrong password or an unknown user fails the handshake alike with `bad_record_mac`, and curl exits 35.",
            "Each of `--tlsuser`, `--tlspassword`, `--srp-verifiers` and `--srp-groups` without its partner, `--tlsauthtype SRP` with no account, no listen URL that uses TLS, a user given twice, and TLS-SRP beside `--http3`, `--cacert`, `--tls-max` below 1.2, `--tlsv1.3`, `--cert` or `--self-signed` are refused, exit 2. A `--srp-verifiers` or `--srp-groups` file that cannot be read, or holds a malformed line, exits 37, naming the file and line.",
        ],
        ["websocket"] =
        [
            "The WebSocket server (RFC 6455) answers the HTTP/1.1 upgrade `GET` upstream curl sends for `ws://` and `wss://` URLs with `101 Switching Protocols`, then sends the file at the request path as one binary message, or a directory's listing as one text message when `--list-directories` is given, in frames of at most 65536 payload bytes, then an empty `CLOSE`. `curl ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/<file> -o <local file>` downloads `<file>` that way.",
            "An upgrade that is not one is refused as RFC 6455 says, and curl exits 22: a missing path `404 Not Found`, a method other than GET `405 Method Not Allowed`, a request with no `Upgrade: websocket` or a `Sec-WebSocket-Version` other than 13 `426 Upgrade Required`, and any other malformed upgrade `400 Bad Request`. No subprotocol or extension is ever selected.",
            "`--ws-echo` makes the server an echo server instead: the path is not looked up, surl sends nothing of its own, and every complete client message is sent back whole until the client closes. It is how a libcurl program driving `curl_ws_send` and `curl_ws_recv` gets answers.",
            "The upgrade is judged as an HTTP GET (see the auth topic): with no account configured it needs no login; once any account is configured it needs one, and curl logs in with Basic (`curl -u`), Bearer (`curl --oauth2-bearer`) or AWS Signature Version 4 (`curl --aws-sigv4`), which it sends unasked. Basic and Bearer over `ws://` are refused unchecked unless `--allow-plaintext-auth` is given. curl does not answer a `401`, so Digest, NTLM and Negotiate, though offered, end with exit 22.",
            "Once upgraded, a `PING` is answered with a `PONG` and a client `CLOSE` with a `CLOSE` echoing its code. A client frame or message past `--max-message` is answered `CLOSE` 1009 and an invalid one 1002 or 1007, and an exchange past `--idle-timeout` or `--max-time` `CLOSE` 1001. The upgrade head is bounded by `--max-request-head` and `--head-timeout`, answered 431 and 408 as the HTTP server answers them.",
            "`wss` is the same server over TLS from the first byte, so a `wss` listen URL needs `--cert` or `--self-signed` (see the tls topic).",
